# Catalog compatibility design

Authority: README.md and legacy/INTEGRATION.md; local execution already authorized.

Preserve both tables and original legacy bytes during migration. Validate every candidate, reconcile by version, and reject divergent ties. Read both candidates in a single SQLite snapshot. New writes use an immediate transaction to read the latest version, reject overflow, and publish both formats atomically.

Migration uses ascending id keyset pagination with bounded payload batches, each inside an immediate transaction. Start each invocation from the beginning so edits to already visited ids are picked up on retry. Invoke callbacks only after commit, including a partial final batch. No persistent progress marker is needed.

Alternatives: destructive conversion violates coexistence; a once-only checkpoint misses desktop edits; a long transaction prevents between-batch desktop writes. Selected approach bounds memory by batch payload size and holds SQLite's writer lock for one batch. No background service, schema rename, dependency, or legacy producer change.

Non-goals: migration across multiple simultaneous service calls; the service serializes synchronous calls. SQLite busy_timeout remains 1000ms and lock/storage failures propagate.
EOF
